Lend the keyboard to the window the application types into

With -u the application keeps the keyboard, and a window of another
client that is shown under it gets the pointer and touches and nothing
else. That is as it should be for the keys of the machine's keyboards. It
leaves an application that has an on-screen keyboard of its own with no
way to type into such a window with it: the virtual-keyboard protocol,
which Cage offers, sends keys to whoever has the keyboard focus, and that
is the application itself.

Now the application can lend the focus. For as long as it has a virtual
keyboard of its own - one made by the client Cage was started for - the
window it has on show under its own has the keyboard focus, and what that
virtual keyboard types goes to it. If no window is on show, or more than
one is, side by side, there is nobody to say a key is for: the focus
stays with the application, and what its virtual keyboard types is
dropped.

Nothing but that virtual keyboard reaches the window. While the focus is
lent, what every other keyboard does - the machine's own, and any other
client's virtual one - is passed to no client; Cage's own key bindings
still answer. The window is told of no key that was down when it was
given the focus, and a press on the application's window, where the keys
of an on-screen keyboard are, does not take the focus back.

The focus comes back to the application when it destroys its virtual
keyboard, and when the window is hidden, replaced by another or closed;
a window that takes the place of another while the virtual keyboard is
still there is lent the focus in its turn. The group Cage makes for the
application's virtual keyboard is freed with that keyboard, where the
group of any virtual keyboard is left behind: an application that makes
one each time its on-screen keyboard comes up would leave one behind each
time.

This is what lets the crew type into another program's page - a web
page, a map's search box - with the OpenGVA HMI's on-screen keyboard,
from the bezel or by touch.

Upstream: not submitted

